(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the cure rate of catheter ablation for atrioventricular nodal reentry?","choices":["About 50%","About 70%","About 90%","Over 95%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation offers a select subset of patients with nodal reentry the opportunity for a definitive cure, in over 95%. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does the rhythm of multifocal atrial tachycardia compare with focal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["Regular; focal is irregular","Both are regular","Both lack P waves","Irregular; focal is regular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Focal atrial tachycardia is regular because one focus fires; multifocal atrial tachycardia is irregular because at least three foci fire independently. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How do most people with premature junctional contractions present?","choices":["With syncope","With chest pain","With a stroke","Without symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most people with premature junctional contractions are not symptomatic, and no treatment is needed if they are asymptomatic and infrequent; reassurance is often adequate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How common is hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["The usual presentation","Tied to a wide QRS complex","Mainly in older adults","Less common than stable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia is the less common presentation; many patients have no symptoms or only transient ones, and collapse usually needs a rate above 200. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 26"},{"q":"Which is a physiologic cause of sinus tachycardia?","choices":["Pain or anxiety","Hypovolemia","Pulmonary embolism","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pain and anxiety are physiologic causes, as is exercise; the pathologic causes include hypovolemia, shock, anemia, hypoxia, pulmonary embolism, myocardial infarction and hyperthyroidism. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Sinus tachycardia is defined by a sinus rate in which range?","choices":["From 60 to 100","Above 100, up to 160","Above 160","From 250 to 300"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus tachycardia is a regular sinus rhythm above 100 up to 160 beats per minute, with a P wave for every QRS; above 160 the P waves are lost and the rhythm is called supraventricular tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When is catheter ablation used for premature atrial contractions?","choices":["Whenever any are found","When they cause no symptoms","When caffeine triggers them","When they trigger fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation is used if premature atrial contractions are frequent or are triggering atrial fibrillation; otherwise a beta blocker or reassurance is used. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"In hemodynamically stable atrial fibrillation, what is the first aim of management?","choices":["A permanent pacemaker","Rate control","Immediate rhythm conversion","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable atrial fibrillation is managed first with rate control (intravenous if in rapid ventricular response, oral if not), with anticoagulation guided by stroke risk. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which trigger can precipitate atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia?","choices":["Increased vagal tone","Caffeine","Hypothyroidism","Beta blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nodal reentry may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists or sympathomimetics, usually without heart disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Besides athletes and sleepers, which adult group commonly shows sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Adults over 65","Adults with anxiety","Adults with hyperthyroidism","Adults using stimulants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sinus bradycardia is listed as seen in adults over 65 years, as well as in well-conditioned, active people and during sleep. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram pattern defines sinus arrhythmia?","choices":["Irregularly irregular with no P waves","Regular with sawtooth baseline waves","Irregular with three P-wave shapes","Cyclic irregularity, normal P waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sinus arrhythmia is irregular with a pattern that follows breathing, at 60 to 100 beats per minute, with a uniform P wave before every narrow QRS and a normal PR interval. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia?","choices":["Amiodarone","A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atropine is first line for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which drug is used for persistent symptoms from premature junctional contractions?","choices":["Metoprolol","Adenosine","Atropine","Procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If symptoms from premature junctional contractions persist, a beta blocker such as metoprolol is used if not contraindicated; reassurance of the benign rhythm is often adequate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"After how much anticoagulation can stable atrial fibrillation be electively cardioverted as an outpatient?","choices":["24 hours","21 days","3 months","1 year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient cardioversion of atrial fibrillation follows 21 days of anticoagulation; anticoagulation is guided by stroke risk throughout. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia depends on which structure?","choices":["Three atrial foci","A damaged sinus node","An accessory pathway","A circuit inside the node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is seen in patients with an accessory pathway, such as Wolff-Parkinson-White; the loop uses the atrioventricular node and the pathway. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the definitive management of atrial flutter?","choices":["Vagal maneuvers","Ablation","Adenosine","A pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ablation is the definitive management of atrial flutter, which has a substantial recurrence rate and is harder to rate-control than fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which cardiac event is a listed cause of junctional rhythm?","choices":["Hypovolemic shock","Untreated hyperthyroidism","Inferior myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause of junctional rhythm, with sinus node dysfunction, the acute period after cardiac surgery and digoxin toxicity. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"In sick sinus syndrome, a fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves alternates with pauses. Which rhythm is the fast run?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular fast rhythm with no P waves is atrial fibrillation, and in tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which fast and slow rhythms alternate, atrial fibrillation is the most common tachyarrhythmia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"When premature junctional contractions are frequent (over 10 per minute), what is done?","choices":["Check for heart disease","Reassurance alone","Anticoagulation","A permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Frequent premature junctional contractions (more than 10 per minute, or multifocal) prompt a search for structural heart disease and treatment with a beta blocker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"In sinus arrest, which rhythm may take over?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","A junctional rhythm","Atrial flutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In sinus arrest the sinus node does not generate an impulse, and a junctional rhythm may take over, often with inverted (retrograde) P waves or none visible. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Atrial flutter is commonly seen in which patients?","choices":["Raised intracranial pressure","Scarring after heart surgery","Young women using caffeine","Healthy, conditioned athletes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrial flutter is commonly seen with atrial scarring or damage (for example after surgery), right-sided heart failure or pulmonary vascular disease, and in atrial fibrillation patients taking an antiarrhythmic. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is a recognized cause of sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Heavy caffeine intake","Surgical injury to the node","Excess thyroid hormone","Anemia from blood loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Causes of sick sinus syndrome include sinoatrial node ischemia, injury to the node from cardiac surgery, electrolyte abnormalities, medications and hypothyroidism. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the management of asymptomatic sinus bradycardia?","choices":["No treatment","Atropine","A temporary pacemaker","A permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic sinus bradycardia needs no treatment; atropine is for acute, symptomatic, unstable bradycardia and a permanent pacemaker for chronic symptomatic disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does a premature junctional contraction look like?","choices":["Early narrow QRS, P inverted or hidden","Early wide QRS without a P wave","Early, abnormal upright P wave","Sawtooth waves before the QRS"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A premature junctional contraction gives an early narrow QRS with either an inverted P wave, a P wave buried in the QRS (no P visible) or a P wave after the QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which type of atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is most common?","choices":["Orthodromic","Antidromic","Slow-fast","Fast-slow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow-fast (typical) is the most common type of atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia, a regular narrow complex tachycardia with hidden P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How do patients with atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia most commonly present?","choices":["Palpitations","Stroke","Heart failure","Bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is seen in patients with an accessory pathway, and they most commonly present with palpitations. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which step comes before any drug?","choices":["A vagal maneuver","An echocardiogram","Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A vagal maneuver is first line for stable supraventricular (narrow complex) tachycardia, before adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is a delta wave?","choices":["An inverted T wave","A sawtooth baseline","A slurred upstroke of the QRS","A notched, wide P wave"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The delta wave is a slurred upstroke of the QRS complex, seen with a short PR interval and a widened QRS in Wolff-Parkinson-White. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which pair are both listed risk factors for atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Young age and female sex","Low weight and anemia","Hypertension and diabetes","Hypothyroidism and hypotension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension and diabetes are both listed, along with cardiac disease, family history, obesity, sleep apnea, excessive alcohol and older age. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 appears in someone with a known accessory pathway. Which intravenous drug is used?","choices":["Adenosine","Atropine","Magnesium","Procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia approaching 300 with an accessory pathway is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, treated with intravenous procainamide, never adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
